WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.901
Path traversal occurs when untrusted input
influences file system access outside the

2
00:00:04.981 --> 00:00:07.151
location the application intended to use.

3
00:00:07.231 --> 00:00:10.969
In C, that usually means some path-like
value eventually reaches an open,

4
00:00:11.049 --> 00:00:13.220
read, write, rename, delete, extraction,

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.279
or similar file operation without enough
control over where that operation is

6
00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:18.203
allowed to land.

7
00:00:18.283 --> 00:00:21.900
The important point is that path traversal
is not only a string problem.

8
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.668
It is an access boundary problem.

9
00:00:23.748 --> 00:00:27.908
A program may have a perfectly valid
string that still points outside the approved

10
00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:28.712
storage area.

11
00:00:28.792 --> 00:00:33.012
It may also have a string that looked safe
earlier but resolves differently because

12
00:00:33.092 --> 00:00:35.986
the file system changed, the working
directory changed,

13
00:00:36.066 --> 00:00:39.140
or platform rules interpreted the text in
an unexpected way.

14
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:42.596
The impact can be serious even when no
network service is involved.

15
00:00:42.676 --> 00:00:45.992
A traversal defect can expose files the
program should not serve,

16
00:00:46.072 --> 00:00:49.629
overwrite data the program should not
modify, damage configuration,

17
00:00:49.709 --> 00:00:53.809
confuse logs or reports, or break
separation between users, tenants, projects,

18
00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:54.612
or workflows.

19
00:00:54.692 --> 00:00:58.611
The application may believe it is
operating inside one managed area while the

20
00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.284
operating system is asked to touch
something else.

21
00:01:01.364 --> 00:01:03.775
Many defects begin with ordinary
assumptions.

22
00:01:03.855 --> 00:01:08.075
A developer assumes an input is only a
filename because the user interface labels it

23
00:01:08.155 --> 00:01:08.638
that way.

24
00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:11.672
A helper assumes the current working
directory is stable.

25
00:01:11.752 --> 00:01:14.766
A service assumes a caller already checked
authorization.

26
00:01:14.846 --> 00:01:18.946
A parser assumes metadata names are
trustworthy because they came from a file the

27
00:01:19.026 --> 00:01:20.232
application accepted.

28
00:01:20.312 --> 00:01:23.688
These assumptions are where path design
needs to become explicit.

29
00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:27.807
Prevention starts by separating trusted
storage policy from untrusted names.

30
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:31.987
The application decides which roots are
approved, which workflows may use them,

31
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:35.081
and what kind of relative names are
acceptable inside them.

32
00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.331
User input can select an allowed resource,

33
00:01:37.411 --> 00:01:39.883
but it should not redefine the storage
boundary.

34
00:01:39.963 --> 00:01:43.460
That distinction is the foundation for
traversal-resistant C code.
